<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- ProMotion is opt in on iPhone. Without this key iOS caps every CAAnimation and
	     CADisplayLink in the app at 60Hz on a 120Hz phone, which is why the app felt
	     half as smooth as the system UI around it. Apple made it opt in for battery,
	     and it only costs anything while something is actually animating. -->
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The app's only cryptography is HTTPS to our own Worker and Apple's own App
	     Attest. Both are exempt, and without this key every single upload stops and
	     asks the question by hand before the build reaches a tester. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Coach Jett</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<!-- The name App Store Connect checks for clashes. It was $(PRODUCT_NAME), which is
	     "Readiness", the project's old name: a dictionary word another app already uses,
	     and not the name on the listing. Apple rejected build 1 over it (ITMS-90129). -->
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Coach Jett</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.coachjett.ios</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>coachjett</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>JettProxyURL</key>
	<string>https://coachjett-proxy.hlincontacts.workers.dev</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Take a meal photo to review and log its estimated calories and macros.</string>
	<key>NSHealthShareUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Coach Jett reads sleep, heart and breathing metrics, temperature, activity, workouts, height, date of birth, biological sex, body measurements, nutrition (including caffeine and alcohol), and mindful sessions to show trends and guide training, sleep, and recovery. You choose which data to allow. With separate permission, derived summaries are shared with DeepSeek for AI coaching.</string>
	<key>NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Save weight measurements and meals you choose to add to Apple Health.</string>
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Coach Jett notices when you have been at your gym for a few minutes so Jett can offer to take the session down while you rest. Your location is only ever compared against gyms you have named yourself, and never leaves this device.</string>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Coach Jett notices when you have been at your gym for a few minutes so Jett can offer to take the session down while you rest. Your location is only ever compared against gyms you have named yourself, and never leaves this device.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Save a share card of your day to Photos.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Say what you just lifted and Coach Jett writes the set down, so you never have to type between sets.</string>
	<key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Coach Jett turns what you say between sets into logged sets. Where your iPhone supports it, the words are recognised on the device and never leave it.</string>
	<key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<!-- Location only. `processing` was here for a BGTaskScheduler job that was never
	     written, and a background mode the app does not use is something App Review
	     rejects rather than ignores. -->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>location</string>
	</array>
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict/>
	<!-- arm64. `armv7` is the old Xcode template value, and it claims this app needs a
	     32-bit device, which nothing running iOS 17 is. -->
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
